Alright… here we are again, huh?  

It seems to me that RabbitMQ can’t really run with such low (weak) hardware requirements…  
However, as my friend Phoenix Wright would say:  

HOLD IT!  

We still have a second option, which, if we think about it, is actually smarter for our project structure.  

We’ve got our eyes on KeyDB, which is already being used by our project as in-memory storage for values such as:  

- Total number of requests made to each of the two services  
- Timestamp of when the last Health Check was performed *(function of the late Doctor)*  

And now, we’re going to use KeyDB to store the messages that would have been sent by RabbitMQ.  
We already have fully decoupled functions that handle sending via the proxy and processing by the workers, so we just need to create a queue in KeyDB and we’re done!  

